Cryptsetup 2.8.9 Release Notes
==============================
Stable security bug-fix release.

All users of cryptsetup must upgrade to this version.

Changes since version 2.8.8
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

This release fixes several bugs found by the OSS Scanner project,
which uses Anthropic AI models. Full unedited descriptions of the reported bugs
(without reproducers and AI-generated patches) can be found in the cryptsetup project issues
(https://gitlab.com/cryptsetup/cryptsetup/-/work_items?state=closed&search=ANT-2026).

Many of the bugs assume that an attacker can modify on-disk metadata and let
the user activate such a crafted device. While this is not the default threat model,
we try to harden the code (extend metadata validation) to eliminate such issues.
Although such a metadata attack can, e.g., weaken encryption for new writes, confidentiality
of existing encrypted data is preserved (decryption will return only garbage).

High severity fixes
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

* Add OPAL segment digest (ANT-2026-HPXPQMWG).

  NOTE: The fix for this issue is backward incompatible (for OPAL HW-encrypted drives only).

  LUKS2 optionally supports hardware-encrypted OPAL drives.
  The LUKS keyslot then contains the OPAL unlocking passphrase instead of the volume key.

  As the metadata entry for the encrypted data segment type is not authenticated, an attacker
  can change a regular LUKS data segment to the OPAL type in LUKS metadata.
  The next activation of such a crafted device will then send the volume key to the underlying
  hardware device. This breaks the LUKS security model, in which, for software encryption,
  the disk is always treated as an untrusted device where only ciphertext is stored.

  To exploit this issue, the attacker also needs to control the underlying device.
  This is mainly a problem in confidential computing environments that require strong
  isolation from the hypervisor. Regular (non-OPAL) drives reject such an operation.

  This issue is an unfortunate design flaw in the LUKS2 OPAL segment definition.
  It is fixed by redesigning the digest for the OPAL data segment type.

  The new version of the OPAL data segment digest is now also calculated from segment
  attributes (such as type and key size), so an attacker can no longer create such a crafted device.

  All new devices formatted with OPAL encryption now use LUKS metadata with OPAL requirement
  version 2. Such devices cannot be opened with older cryptsetup versions.

  Support for older OPAL metadata can be disabled (enforcing the new digest only) at compile time:
    - with autoconf option --enable-enforce-opal-segment-digest, or
    - with meson option: -Denforce-opal-segment-digest=true.

  Metadata can always be upgraded in place with the cryptsetup repair command.

  The next cryptsetup version 2.9 will enforce the new digest by default (old devices will
  not be activated and will require a metadata update with the repair command).

  For metadata changes, please see the updated LUKS2 format specification included in the tarball.

* LUKS2: Validate that the PBKDF2 digest in metadata is complete (ANT-2026-EM3CT2VN).

  A flaw in LUKS2 digest verification allows a crafted LUKS2 header to be accepted
  and partially reintroduces CVE-2021-4122 (a forged "crashed decryption" attack:
  crafted reencryption metadata allowed decryption of a selected region of data on the next
  ordinary unlock). (The issue was introduced in version 2.5.0.)

* Do not accept keyring keys owned by other users (ANT-2026-AIEJAWT4).

  Cryptsetup scans /proc/keys when it cannot resolve a key or keyring by name
  in the caller's own keyrings. This can cause a different key in the keyring to be loaded.
  A local unprivileged user could therefore plant a keyring key that will be used
  by a different user to store the volume key if the --link-vk-to-keyring option is used.

  The code now accepts a keyring key only when it is owned by the calling user.

Fixes for null (empty) cipher detection (security-relevant)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

* Fix null cipher detection if set as null-ecb (ANT-2026-76MNV0CY).

* LUKS1: Harden detection of null cipher (ANT-2026-TFQUDZT2).

  A null cipher can be used only with an empty passphrase, even with LUKS1.

Key size checks hardening (security-relevant)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

* LUKS: Limit minimum key size (ANT-2026-EGL2YP2V).

  Require at least an 8-byte key to avoid accepting crafted images.
  If the key size is set to 1 byte, a random password will unlock the device
  with a probability of 1/256 without any warning.

  Note that such a setting requires a cipher with a variable key size, such as Serpent.
  As the volume key changes, decryption of existing data returns only garbage.

* LUKS2: Limit minimum encryption key size if an integrity profile is used (ANT-2026-WGZ9TGAN).

  In the integrity profile, the encryption and integrity protection keys (e.g., for HMAC)
  are stored as one value. With a variable key size algorithm such as Serpent, metadata
  can be crafted to weaken the integrity key and eliminate integrity protection.

* OPAL: Reject volume keys longer than 255 bytes (ANT-2026-RL4YGVAD).
  Add a check for the maximum OPAL volume key length to prevent overflow.

* OPAL: Fix off-by-one check for key length (ANT-2026-UDAHEINW).

  While the kernel syscall uses a 256-byte buffer, the maximum length is 255.

* BITLK: Reject short clear-key/VMK key entries from metadata (ANT-2026-TD4Y5TFX).

* BITLK: Reject FVEK (volume key) if its size differs from the header (ANT-2026-W0WPK43B).

LUKS metadata validation hardening (security-relevant)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

* LUKS2: Reject duplicate backup segment flags (ANT-2026-G8FJOKAF).

  If the device is in active reencryption and contains additional segment metadata,
  a crafted duplicate backup segment can introduce a null cipher or a similar change
  on the next activation.

* LUKS2: Fix order-independent validation of segments behind the backup segment (ANT-2026-JXTV6TLM).

* LUKS2: Reject backup segments without the requirement flag (ANT-2026-QYA1BDCQ).

  The requirement flag indicates that reencryption is running.
  Without it, backup segments are invalid.

* LUKS: Enforce validation of cipher specification for on-disk metadata (ANT-2026-IA2TNSPO).

  The cipher specification can be used as a device-mapper table parameter, and an intentionally
  crafted specification can corrupt the mapping table (specifically if it contains spaces).

  The cipher and mode specification is now strictly validated to contain only valid characters.

* LUKS: Enforce validation and reject multikey cipher specification (ANT-2026-KF5OJAIT).

  Multikey dm-crypt cipher specifications are never used for valid LUKS devices.

* LUKS2: Reject algorithms with authentication tag without integrity (ANT-2026-X7A1WEAS).

  All allowed integrity-protected algorithms for LUKS2 must use a proper integrity mode.
  Using "none" must be rejected to avoid insecure combinations, possibly created
  by crafted LUKS2 metadata.

* OPAL: Harden OPAL locking range checks before erasing (ANT-2026-TRE9CTGV).

  Check that the configured locking range on the device matches the hw-opal segment configured
  in LUKS2 metadata before running luksErase.

* LUKS2: Fix validation of datashift option in reencryption metadata (ANT-2026-X8MIOXOO).

  With datashift-checksum resilience, the shift-size has to be aligned to a multiple
  of the area sector size.

* LUKS2: Reject wrong JSON object IDs (ANT-2026-UYIESGUA).

  Tokens, keyslots and digests use numeric names with a strict maximum value.
  The name should be in a simple form like "0", without any prefix like "00".

* LUKS2: Reject JSON token metadata nested too deeply (ANT-2026-RYR6LO8H).

* LUKS2: Allow linear data segment only for hw-opal devices (ANT-2026-QGXBEPOR).

Other security-relevant fixes
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

* Wipe area between LUKS2 header and data offset after encryption (ANT-2026-P8F3E3LW).

  After in-place encryption, wipe the area between LUKS2 metadata and the new data offset,
  as it could contain stale plaintext data.

* Reject invalid log file for LUKS1 offline reencryption (ANT-2026-I0244IJM).

* veritysetup: Do not overwrite verification with FEC repair status (ANT-2026-SFC4NG8Z).

  If verification failed and an FEC (Forward Error Correction) image was provided,
  the verification result was replaced with the FEC repair status.

  The FEC repairable status was meant as a hint, so the whole operation now uses only
  the hash verification status and reports only an advisory FEC message.

* plain: Fix hashing of passphrase if variable-size hash is used (ANT-2026-ECGSC1G5).

  A variable-size hash caused a failure that was not detected.

* LUKS2: Do not grow (invalid) LUKS2 images during reencryption (ANT-2026-SUCSFBPX).

  Reject invalid LUKS2 images (files smaller than the LUKS2 data offset) early and do not
  accidentally grow them to the data offset stored in LUKS2 metadata.

* verity: Do not enlarge hash file during verification (ANT-2026-J1JUL9XO).

  Verification must not touch hash images, only use stored data.

* LUKS2: Wipe temporary reencryption LUKS header files before removal (ANT-2026-XO7DALE6).

  Overwrite the header file with random data before unlinking it.

* Harden reencryption hotzone checks before crash recovery (ANT-2026-OERDOWB7).

  The hotzone offset and length from the metadata must be checked against
  the real size of the data device.

* Detect volume key keyring collisions between same-UUID contexts (ANT-2026-ANZY284A).

  The kernel keyring key description used for a LUKS2 volume key is derived
  from the device UUID. When two crypt_device contexts in the same thread
  operate on distinct devices that share a UUID, a key uploaded later by
  one context replaces (shadows) the key previously uploaded by the other.

  Using two LUKS2 devices with the same UUID is an unsupported configuration.
  The code now fails if such a collision is detected.

  The collision cannot happen with standard system utilities (these utilities do not
  use threads for activation).

* LUKS1: Force reset PBKDF to PBKDF2 on load and format API calls (ANT-2026-S8SVEDK4).

  If the user explicitly sets PBKDF to Argon2 (not supported for LUKS1) before a metadata
  load operation, PBKDF2 could end up with default settings (1000 iterations).
  This requires a very specific order of API operations (not used in cryptsetup).

* Invalidate volume key loaded by another context on suspend/deactivate (ANT-2026-BXEJ9Q4N).

  If the device was activated by one context that is still kept in memory,
  and it was later suspended or deactivated through a second context, the
  key was not dropped from the keyring.

  Fix it by invalidating the volume key in the kernel keyring when it is still reachable
  from the calling process but cannot be unlinked from the current context's keyring.

* OPAL: Do not store Admin1 PIN on the stack (ANT-2026-XY6O9WYT).

  Use safe memory instead.

* LUKS2: Fix possible double free in reencryption step (ANT-2026-N0710LP2).

* TCRYPT: Lock decrypted header memory (ANT-2026-P6N65ETW).

  The TCRYPT decrypted header contains encryption keys, so handle it as key material.

* Validate device in internal device helper on reopen (ANT-2026-FFOPB8HP).

  The internal device helper should validate that a newly opened descriptor points to the same
  device/file as the cached file descriptor.

* LUKS2: Fix error path in LUKS2 token processing (ANT-2026-X7WXQ9J0).

  In some specific situations, LUKS2 token unlock returned success
  without allocating the key. Return failure in such a case.

* Always wipe device-mapper parameters table buffer (ANT-2026-BHXQHFIH).

  Mapping table parameters can contain key material.

* BITLK: Fix parsing of metadata with missing FVEK and VMK entries (ANT-2026-XQGBJCZV).

  Fixes a NULL dereference when parsing devices with these keys missing.

* Refuse LUKS2 to LUKS1 conversion when salt is not LUKS_SALTSIZE bytes (ANT-2026-BGUYYG1L).

  Fail the conversion if the decoded salt length is incorrect.

Other regular bug fixes
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

* LUKS1: Avoid possible overflow in device offset calculations (ANT-2026-VS7IELK0).

* verity: Fail on partial read/write of parity bytes (ANT-2026-UV34BH2W).

* Reject key from keyring if its payload has a different size (ANT-2026-TBEUPMOU).

* Fix error returned in crypt_wipe_device to be negative errno (ANT-2026-S5DZP6KQ).

* Avoid using uninitialized memory in write_lseek_blockwise (ANT-2026-Q03W7V15).

* verity: Reject salt size larger than superblock salt field (ANT-2026-JMBB0A9W).

* LUKS1: Reject conversion from LUKS2 if integrity profile is used (ANT-2026-IL1V8134).

* LUKS2: Reject keyslot if it cannot fit into keyslot area (ANT-2026-H2SH9JCE).

* cryptsetup: Properly fail if no keyslot is available for checking (ANT-2026-GPTLKHBD).

* verity: Properly reinitialize context if already set on reload (ANT-2026-E73D430W).

* FVAULT2: Do not print volume key to terminal if keyfile is requested (ANT-2026-CV4ZFKER).

* TCRYPT: Properly fail if no PBKDF variant can be used (ANT-2026-AFK35TK1).

* LUKS2: Reject reencryption if integrity profile is used (ANT-2026-3FHALHXU).

* Do not use a possibly deallocated PBKDF2 type (ANT-2026-30185VH0).

* BITLK: Wipe recovery key and kdf after use (ANT-2026-E7DZOCRM).

* BITLK: Process key file as binary array (regression in 2.8.1) (ANT-2026-28IC8PZP).

* LUKS1: Reject data offset that cannot fit into a 32-bit integer (ANT-2026-C4OQQQA5).

* LUKS2: Wipe buffer for header backup (ANT-2026-ACZ5BYDZ).

* integritysetup: Set the deallocated journal integrity key pointer to NULL (ANT-2026-7QDRANDJ).

* Fix setting of LUKS2 labels if used with crypt_get_label (ANT-2026-4O3O0R5R).

* LoopAES: Fix off-by-one keyfile size processing (ANT-2026-2M94362Y).

* veritysetup: Fix error path for invalid root hash file (ANT-2026-08GARYXF).

* verity: Work around false positive clang warning in RS decode.

* integrity: Drop stray 's' in sscanf format.

* LUKS2: Fix regenerating binary header salt if one header copy is corrupted.

* OPAL: Always print error for cryptsetup erase with wrong password.

* Add asserts for device locking to silence cppcheck warnings.

* Remove tests that depend on the AF_ALG interface, which can be disabled.

